 "equations": [
  {
   "name": "External resistance for a time interval",
   "expr": "REXT = 100 × (−b + √(b² − 4·a·(c − 100·T))) / (2·a)",
   "where": {
    "REXT": "resistance from DELAY/M_DRV to GND, Ω",
    "T": "wanted interval, s",
    "a, b, c": "coefficients for the range T falls in, from the coefficient table"
   },
   "note": "Worked case: T = 8 s uses set 2 and gives 10.18 kΩ.",
   "page": 12
  },
  {
   "name": "Quantization error",
   "expr": "Err = 100 × (TDESIRED − TADC) / TDESIRED;  TADC = INT[(a·RD² + b·RD + c) / 100];  RD = REXT / 100",
   "where": {
    "Err": "interval error, %",
    "TDESIRED": "wanted interval, s",
    "TADC": "interval the part actually produces from REXT, s",
    "REXT": "resistance from the REXT equation, Ω",
    "a, b, c": "the same coefficient set as used for REXT"
   },
   "note": "",
   "page": 14
  },
  {
   "name": "Uncertainty of two resistors in parallel",
   "expr": "uR∥ = R∥ × √((uR1/R1)² + (uR2/R2)²)",
   "where": {
    "uR∥": "uncertainty of the parallel combination",
    "R∥": "value of the parallel combination",
    "uR1, uR2": "uncertainty of each resistor"
   },
   "note": "",
   "page": 15
  },
  {
   "name": "Uncertainty of one resistor",
   "expr": "uRn = Rn × Tolerance / √3",
   "where": {
    "Rn": "resistor value (n = 1, 2)",
    "Tolerance": "resistor tolerance"
   },
   "note": "",
   "page": 15
  },
  {
   "name": "Supply current while a SPST manual power-on is held",
   "expr": "I = VDD / REXT",
   "where": {
    "VDD": "supply voltage",
    "REXT": "interval resistor"
   },
   "note": "",
   "page": 12
  }
 ],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Overview",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "A nano-power timer with a MOSFET gate driver, made to switch a microcontroller's supply off between tasks. With EN/ONE_SHOT high (timer mode) it pulls DRV low once per interval to turn on a P-channel switch; when the microcontroller answers on DONE the switch is turned off at once, otherwise it stays on for tDRV. With EN/ONE_SHOT low (one-shot) it does this a single time, after power-up. The interval range is 100 ms to 7200 s, and the part draws 35 nA typical at 2.5 V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Start-up",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "After power-on reset the part measures the resistor on DELAY/M_DRV once, by forcing a constant current into it; this takes tR_EXT (100 ms typical, 120 ms max) and draws 200 µA typical. It then enters timer or one-shot mode as EN/ONE_SHOT says. EN/ONE_SHOT must be fixed at a supply level, VDD or GND, by a direct connection. DRV sits high once the reading is done."
  },
  {
   "topic": "DRV output",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "DRV low turns the MOSFET on. Each DRV low pulse starts with the interval and lasts the interval less 50 ms, or less if DONE arrives first. With no DONE, DRV returns high for the final 50 ms so the MOSFET is off before the next interval starts. A manual power-on request is ignored while DRV is low."
  },
  {
   "topic": "DONE input",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "DONE acts on its rising edge; the minimum pulse is 100 ns. Only the first DONE within an interval counts. On DONE the part drives DRV high, 100 ns to tDRV after the DRV falling edge."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Timer mode",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "With EN/ONE_SHOT high, DRV falls once every tIP, measured falling edge to falling edge. The length of each low pulse is ended by DONE, or by the forced rise 50 ms before the next interval if DONE is missed."
  },
  {
   "topic": "One-shot mode",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "With EN/ONE_SHOT low, a single DRV low pulse of the programmed length follows the resistor reading. A further pulse comes only from an accepted manual power-on, and each such pulse again ends early if DONE comes within the interval less 50 ms."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Setting the interval",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "REXT between DELAY/M_DRV and ground selects the interval. It must lie between 500 Ω and 170 kΩ, and a resistor of 1 % or better is recommended. Two standard values in parallel can approach the calculated value; the tables list such pairs."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Manual MOSFET power-on",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "After the resistor reading, DELAY/M_DRV becomes a digital input. Taking it to VDD (above 1.5 V) for at least 20 ms, within a 30 ms observation window, counts as a manual power-on; both edges are de-bounced (20 ms), so short glitches are rejected. DRV then goes low within 10 ms for the programmed interval less 50 ms, or until DONE. The request resets all counters, which start again when the pin returns low; the timing of that DRV pulse is uncertain by about ±5 ms. Holding the pin high keeps the MOSFET on beyond the interval, and DONE is ignored while the pin is high. A request while DRV is already low is ignored. A request during power-on reset or the resistor reading stops the reading, which restarts when the switch is released."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Manual power-on switch choices",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "A momentary switch does the job. A single-pole switch from VDD to DELAY/M_DRV with REXT left in place is the cheaper choice, but draws VDD/REXT while pressed. A changeover switch that takes DELAY/M_DRV off REXT and onto VDD draws no current during the request, for the lowest power."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Interval quantization",
   "page": 14,
   "text": "There are 1650 discrete intervals: nine of them are 100 ms to 900 ms in 100 ms steps, and 1641 cover 1 s to 7200 s. Any wanted interval therefore carries a quantization error, found from the TADC equation."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Interval error from resistor tolerance",
   "page": 14,
   "text": "To find the interval spread from a real resistor: work out REXT_MIN and REXT_MAX from the chosen standard values and their tolerances, turn each into TADC_MIN and TADC_MAX through the TADC expression, and take the errors against the wanted interval. Example: 600 s needs 57.44 kΩ, made from 107 kΩ and 124 kΩ at 1 %. The pair's uncertainty is 0.82 %, so REXT spans 56.96 kΩ to 57.90 kΩ, giving 586.85 s to 611.3 s, an error of -1.88 % / +2.19 %. The range is lopsided because the resistance-to-time conversion is quadratic."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Sensor node application",
   "page": 16,
   "text": "In a battery humidity and temperature node reading every 30 s to 60 s, the TPL5110 removes power from the radio microcontroller between readings rather than letting it count time itself, so the system current falls to tens of nA while idle. The push-button wakes the node for debugging. A low-power regulator and a low-leakage MOSFET are needed to keep the battery life long; the interval is chosen from each device's consumption in its modes."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Power supply",
   "page": 17,
   "text": "Supply 1.8 V to 5.5 V, with a 0.1 µF multilayer ceramic X7R capacitor from VDD to GND."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Layout",
   "page": 17,
   "text": "DELAY/M_DRV is sensitive to stray capacitance, which can upset the interval reading at start-up, so the traces from REXT to ground are kept as short as possible. A short trace from DRV to the MOSFET gate keeps the DRV edges clean. EN/ONE_SHOT goes to its supply level over a short trace."
  }
 ],
